      "id": 1,
      "text": "West Richland officially incorporated on June 17, 1955, after merging the neighboring towns of Heminger City and Enterprise.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Richland%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1948, Carl Heminger purchased 80 acres and laid out a planned community that later became Heminger City.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Richland%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Originally part of Oregon territory, the West Richland area joined Washington Territory in 1853, where settlers harvested sagebrush and river willow.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://westrichland.org/223/City-History",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Lewis and Clark trekked through the region in 1805, and a 1841 Corps of Topographical Engineers survey mapped the Yakima Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Richland%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Unlike nearby Richland, West Richland did not receive the wartime population boom from the Hanford Nuclear Reservation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historylink.org/File/10441",
